Cell["\<\
TGraph constructors have a few well-defined naming conventions, as described \
above for general Tessellatica objects. Make<class> returns an object of type \
<class>.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.595189367381529*^9, 3.595189418929231*^9}, {
  3.59518968484359*^9, 
  3.59518970061664*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"863f9602-4aef-4538-8faa-\
c1bff2394767"],

Cell["\<\
Class TGraph has many subclasses and we define various functions that will \
return instances of one or more subclass. So as to avoid rampant name \
expansion, we don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t work the full class name of the returned \
type into the function name. All of these will have the form \
Make<qualifiers>Graph, where <qualifiers> describes what the return object \
is. But it won\[CloseCurlyQuote]t include class name qualifiers like \
Assigned, Plane, 2D, or 3D (unless there\[CloseCurlyQuote]s actually both 2D \
and 3D flavors of the same thing). So you\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll need to read \
the function docs to determine what class is specifically returned. Such \
names will not include the T; you\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll only see that for a \
specific class\[CloseCurlyQuote]s Make constructor. So:
\tMakeTSomethingGraph \[LongDash] is a Make constructor for class \
TSomethingGraph.
\tMakeSomethingElseGraph \[LongDash] is a Make function that returns some \
subclass of TGraph.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.595189448728963*^9, 3.595189564145653*^9}, {
  3.5951895943173947`*^9, 3.595189647684039*^9}, {3.595189707561063*^9, 
  3.595189824265719*^9}, {3.706824912646976*^9, 
  3.706824917486294*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2d682e26-59ba-49ca-b90c-\
fd8bf05b3b01"]
}, Closed]]
}, Closed]],

