Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Symbolic styles", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.457792797643765*^9, 3.457792801273968*^9}, 
   3.457868868734682*^9},ExpressionUUID->"20963638-64b3-48e4-ba8b-\
c7b64d659bfe"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Origami symbolic styles", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.4426050171977577`*^9, 3.442605028330965*^9}, 
   3.443218264199531*^9, {3.456535783349*^9, 3.456535785410112*^9}, {
   3.4576128588919086`*^9, 3.457612859922312*^9}, 
   3.592208856685751*^9},ExpressionUUID->"98ec52d8-3dae-4e3b-87ab-\
6e047c6cd4bf"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Notes", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.456355656201287*^9, 3.456355664687249*^9}, {
  3.45651796834163*^9, 
  3.456517968777745*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"5ad3a567-edcd-4e6e-8d4b-\
e244ffa7b40a"],

Cell["\<\
In general, we will create graphical images of crease patterns and origami \
objects by using the Style directive, giving a symbolic argument to the Style \
function that represents the type of graphical object. Thus, a valley fold \
line in a crease pattern will be represented as Style[Line[{0,0},{1,0}], \
ValleyLine]. Graphical objects are always created with symbolic styles and \
then we replace the symbolic name using a set of rules that controls the \
rendering. The basic set of rules is OrigamiStyle, which is sufficient to \
create crease patterns and folded forms with a standard appearance. It is \
relatively easy to customize OrigamiStyle.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.456355668615047*^9, 3.456355887656244*^9}, {
  3.456535872767014*^9, 3.456535897195037*^9}, {3.457792890635827*^9, 
  3.457792911931274*^9}, {3.592854008714951*^9, 3.592854071356134*^9}, {
  3.693872238990041*^9, 
  3.693872239062437*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9687a652-c2e6-4347-babf-\
59a2b0865f8e"],

Cell["\<\
Special types of style sets can render lines in different ways. For example, \
ScoringStyle produces lines in colors suitable for laser scoring.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.45635588951186*^9, 3.456355908654982*^9}, {
  3.457792931419998*^9, 3.457792956778295*^9}, {3.592854063044135*^9, 
  3.592854063291764*^9}, {3.628949994662075*^9, 
  3.628950028971444*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b53b2c1b-13ad-41e1-a21a-\
9df09e42743d"],

Cell["\<\
We can also take patterns that are defined with symbolic styles and process \
them further; an example is taking a crease pattern and converting the \
various types of fold lines to Dashified lines for laser scoring.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.45635591090265*^9, 3.4563559790981693`*^9}, {
  3.628949974538228*^9, 
  3.6289499826788063`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"3866db33-8061-46f7-8fb8-\
361ee946c652"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Symbolic line styles:
\tMountainLine
\tValleyLine
\tCreaseLine
\tBorderLine
\tFoldedLine
\tUnfoldedLine
\tHiddenBorderLine
\tHiddenFoldedLine
\tHiddenUnfoldedLine
\tMountainRulingLine
\tValleyRulingLine
\tUndefinedRulingLine
\tNoLine\
\>", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.442605039814452*^9, 3.442605062924668*^9}, {
   3.442605117575748*^9, 3.442605122284069*^9}, {3.442605350198839*^9, 
   3.4426053523337*^9}, {3.442606075273849*^9, 3.442606077866903*^9}, {
   3.44260741646181*^9, 3.442607418228901*^9}, {3.442607493742045*^9, 
   3.4426075219747543`*^9}, 3.456361698744701*^9, {3.456513306078079*^9, 
   3.456513311285557*^9}, {3.457633970082473*^9, 3.457633972039536*^9}, {
   3.457634013919985*^9, 3.457634022665289*^9}, {3.457663294291436*^9, 
   3.4576632960804*^9}, {3.457703803018021*^9, 3.457703812493214*^9}, 
   3.4577061510546703`*^9, {3.484217684970832*^9, 3.4842176864477654`*^9}, {
   3.595200418330312*^9, 3.595200436575192*^9}, {3.628948525065184*^9, 
   3.628948574658678*^9}, 3.628948736910331*^9, 3.6289487722823777`*^9, {
   3.801763322728043*^9, 3.8017633611758757`*^9}, {3.8017634068963957`*^9, 
   3.801763407559999*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b332f65e-87d4-4edc-a6fb-\
22441e5edbc4"],

Cell["\<\
Symbolic line styles are symbols that go into Style[Line..] directives that \
dictate what role the given line plays in an image. In general, there are \
three types of image: a crease pattern (showing the unfolded paper), a 2D \
folded form (showing a flat-folded figure), and a 3D folded form (showing a \
general 3D figure). The symbols and their usages are as follows.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.442605073774269*^9, 3.442605099885703*^9}, {
   3.44260513781573*^9, 3.442605158272208*^9}, {3.44260532015723*^9, 
   3.4426053812468777`*^9}, {3.442606062152798*^9, 3.442606210113533*^9}, {
   3.44260743372229*^9, 3.442607444609962*^9}, {3.442607533288205*^9, 
   3.442607557119468*^9}, {3.456361428546453*^9, 3.456361435985384*^9}, {
   3.457633992943547*^9, 3.4576340620366096`*^9}, {3.457703754173491*^9, 
   3.457703779258663*^9}, {3.4842176889291697`*^9, 3.484217732889811*^9}, {
   3.484241410953783*^9, 3.484241417304348*^9}, 3.6289483434922047`*^9, {
   3.628948394478178*^9, 3.628948397502108*^9}, 3.628948525116744*^9, {
   3.628948591970993*^9, 3.628948596040656*^9}, {3.628948627207439*^9, 
   3.628948659172638*^9}, {3.628948737017234*^9, 3.628948737139002*^9}, 
   3.628948772397278*^9, {3.628948831363068*^9, 3.628949144349172*^9}, 
   3.6289491760907793`*^9, {3.628950190617383*^9, 3.6289501952889643`*^9}, {
   3.689248737242418*^9, 
   3.689248748781493*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"d0bd0e66-412c-4cfd-ba10-\
6840908c5926"],

Cell["\<\
BorderLine -- in either CP or FF, for the border of the paper
ValleyLine -- in a CP, for valley fold lines (ditto)
MountainLine -- in a CP, for mountan fold lines (as viewed from the white \
side of the paper)
CreaseLine -- in a CP, for unfolded crease lines
FoldedLine -- in a FF, a folded line that can be either mountain or valley
UnfoldedLine -- in a FF, an unfolded line or fold line of zero fold angle or \
ruling of zero curvature
HiddenBorderLine -- in a 2D FF, showing xray view of hidden border edges
HiddenFoldedLine -- in a 2D FF, showing xray view of hidden folded edges
HiddenUnfoldedLine -- in a 2D FF, showing xray view of hiddent unfolded edges
ValleyRulingLine -- in a CP, a ruling line with positive ruling curvature
MountainRulingLine -- in a CP, a ruling line with positive ruling curvature
UndefinedRulingLine -- in a CP or FF, a ruling line with undefined ruling \
curvature
NoLine -- don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t show, used to erase an existing line (as a \
style, it will eliminate what it is applied to)\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.689248751690114*^9, {3.689248837848481*^9, 3.689249069345644*^9}, {
   3.801763300411997*^9, 3.8017633015286922`*^9}, {3.801763366691611*^9, 
   3.801763415872505*^9}, {3.801763507145166*^9, 
   3.801763516848503*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"bb0f4844-6a10-42e1-8192-\
c598cf562ad9"],

Cell["\<\
Surface orientation is always assumed that \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]up\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] is the white side of the paper. So crease patterns \
are presented from the white side, and mountain/valley status is defined \
relative to the white side in both crease patterns and folded forms.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.689248762727805*^9, 
  3.689248835416285*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"bf06eab6-0e9d-4227-8bdb-\
0f0a636d17db"],

Cell["\<\
Note that when rendering Graphics3D objects, lines are rendered the same way \
whether they are being viewed from the white side or the colored side. So you \
should generally not use ValleyLine, etc., in Graphics3D patterns, only \
FoldedLine and UnfoldedLine. Typically one uses UnfoldedLine for rulings in \
the FF.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.689259429426767*^9, 
  3.689259517465765*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1d46a8e6-97e6-4e55-b1cf-\
037eedec2de6"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"BorderLine", "::", "usage"}], 
   "=", "\"\<BorderLine is a symbolic line style indicating the border of the \
paper in either crease pattern or folded form.\>\""}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.591800065310281*^9, 3.59180011158897*^9}, {
  3.628949266991171*^9, 3.628949272462369*^9}},
 CellLabel->
  "In[1530]:=",ExpressionUUID->"5eadbf35-e873-483d-bd01-76b86380d5af"],

