Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Unit quadrilateral centered split twist tiles", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.630149335188027*^9, 
  3.630149379199472*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"9ad3f364-ce98-4917-85a3-\
f9e333813754"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Notes", "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.6301503932152767`*^9, 
  3.630150393661385*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"18268015-e459-4f52-871d-\
36c7dff94b90"],

Cell["\<\
The three types of unit quadrilateral split twist tile can create a split \
twist in any quadrilateral  as long as the central polygon is not \
self-crossing or retrograde. You have to specify the degree-6 vertex \
(parameter ip), which must be one of the Kawasaki-excess vertices of the \
central polygon. So you need to start with a little research: generate a \
PolygonCenteredTwistTile or PolygonOffsetTwistTile TGraph from the \
QuadrilateralVertices; examine the twist graph to verify non-self-crossing; \
then examine the corner angles using PolygonTwistCentralPolygonAnglesExcess \
to see which corners are eligible to be the degree-6 vertex. Then, there is \
no guarantee of non-self-intersection, so you\[CloseCurlyQuote]ll also need \
to examine the crease assignment to see if there is a non-flat-foldable \
version.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.630150395632607*^9, 3.630150540540755*^9}, {
  3.630150572699718*^9, 3.630150705537278*^9}, {3.630151608840165*^9, 
  3.6301516487735176`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"24443a64-084b-4ceb-8dd8-\
17b4e8708c83"]
}, Closed]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[TextData[{
 "{UnitQuadrilateralCenteredSplitTwistTile, {",
 StyleBox["angles, side, is}, w, \[Tau]",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["ip",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["ctype",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["types",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "}"
}], "Subsubsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.629850113433739*^9, 3.629850127527081*^9}, {
   3.629850200481357*^9, 3.6298502046158133`*^9}, {3.6298502604392033`*^9, 
