   3.627913623183637*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"bd792e2e-6de3-4a75-aa90-\
48966a20de33"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "VeneerScoringCarveGraphics[",
 StyleBox["tobj",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", ",
 StyleBox["opts",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "] takes a TGraph+TAssigned ",
 StyleBox["tobj",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " representing the crease pattern and a list ",
 StyleBox["foldangles",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " representing the fold angles of the pattern, and generates a Graphics \
object that represents a veneer scoring pattern. This function uses absolute \
line width to generate the kerf, which works, but requires one use the \
\[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]carve\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] mode (raster-scanning) \
of the laser cutter, which is much less efficient than pure vector-writing. \
Options are:\n\tImageSize \[Rule] ",
 StyleBox["size",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", where size is the desired width of the pattern in points. Use this value \
as the ImageSize option when exporting the file.\n\tScoreLineThickness \
\[Rule] ",
 StyleBox["ht",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", where ",
 StyleBox["ht",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " is the width of a hairline (ordinary cut line) in points\n\t\
VeneerThickness \[Rule] ",
 StyleBox["vt",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", where ",
 StyleBox["vt",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " is the veneer thickness, also given in points (for unit consistency).\n\t\
InvertFolds \[Rule] True | False inverts the crease assignment in the pattern."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.624889911255558*^9, 3.624889929508737*^9}, {
   3.62488996334678*^9, 3.624890164533807*^9}, {3.624890203483369*^9, 
   3.6248902196181297`*^9}, 3.624891358509203*^9, {3.6248946868523912`*^9, 
   3.624894752743634*^9}, {3.624895696508016*^9, 3.6248957587535973`*^9}, 
   3.624913968169517*^9, {3.6249154746598673`*^9, 3.62491553531007*^9}, 
   3.625164208159892*^9, {3.6251652412597733`*^9, 3.625165244874075*^9}, 
   3.626360974750469*^9, {3.626361915679893*^9, 
   3.626361929036378*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a880e95a-42d9-4c80-be50-\
8b8a3776c5e4"],

